reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:1:// reference/src/semantics/float.k — MPY-FLOAT
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:2://
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:3:// Minimal, TRUSTED float support. K/Haskell (kprove) has no usable symbolic IEEE-754 theory and
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:4:// no Int2Float hook, so a float value stays an OPAQUE proof-domain primitive: `intFloatDiv(I, F)`
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:5:// (the Python true division I / F) is `[no-evaluators]` for kprove — it never reduces on symbolic
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:6:// input, and the proof verifies only the STRUCTURE (the result is the exact term the program
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:7:// builds). The `[concrete]` rule evaluates it for krun (llvm HAS the Int2Float / /Float hooks), so
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:8:// smoke tests run the real float value.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:9://
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:10:// Only the pieces needed so far: Float as a Val, the float literal, and Int / float true division
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:11:// (the `a * h / 2.0` idiom). More float ops (float +/-/*, comparison) are added as float problems
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:12:// need them — kept minimal + sound.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:14:module MPY-FLOAT
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:19:  // Float is a value; the float literal evaluates to the K Float.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:20:  syntax Val ::= Float
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:21:  rule <k> Float(F:Float) => F ... </k>
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:23:  // Int / float true division. OPAQUE for kprove (no-evaluators); concrete for krun.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:24:  syntax Float ::= intFloatDiv(Int, Float) [function, total, symbol(intFloatDiv), no-evaluators]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:25:  rule intFloatDiv(I:Int, F:Float) => Int2Float(I, 53, 11) /Float F [concrete]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:27:  rule applyBin("/", I:Int, F:Float) => intFloatDiv(I, F)
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:29:  // Int / Int true division (Python: always a float). OPAQUE for kprove; concrete for krun.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:30:  syntax Float ::= divII(Int, Int) [function, total, symbol(divII), no-evaluators]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:31:  rule divII(I1:Int, I2:Int) => Int2Float(I1, 53, 11) /Float Int2Float(I2, 53, 11) [concrete]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:32:  rule applyBin("/", I1:Int, I2:Int) => divII(I1, I2)
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:34:  // float % float (e.g. `number % 1.0` = the fractional part). OPAQUE for kprove, concrete for
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:35:  // krun. Python's float `%` is floor-based: a % b = a - floor(a/b)*b (K's %Float is IEEE
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:36:  // remainder, which differs — 3.5 %Float 1.0 = -0.5 vs Python 0.5 — so it is NOT used).
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:37:  syntax Float ::= floatMod(Float, Float) [function, total, symbol(floatMod), no-evaluators]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:38:  rule floatMod(F1:Float, F2:Float) => F1 -Float (floorFloat(F1 /Float F2) *Float F2) [concrete]
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:39:  rule applyBin("%", F1:Float, F2:Float) => floatMod(F1, F2)
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:41:  // float equality — for concrete krun asserts (e.g. `area == 7.5`); the FLOAT.eq hook is fine on
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:42:  // concrete floats. kprove proofs return floats structurally and do not compare them.
reference-semantics/semantics/float.k:43:  rule applyCmp("==", F1:Float, F2:Float) => F1 ==Float F2
